From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 00:31:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] ALSA: hda/hdmi: store channel allocation derived values once

init_channel_allocations() recomputes the derived fields of the global
channel_allocations[] table each time a codec registers its chmap ops
(snd_hdac_register_chmap_ops()). It stores zero first and then
increments the fields in memory for each speaker (the compiled code is a
plain "addl $1, (mem)" per speaker). When two HDMI codecs register at
the same time, one call can reset an entry while the other is counting,
and the entry is left with a channel count anywhere between the real
one and twice it.

This was hit on a desktop with a discrete NVIDIA GPU and an AMD iGPU,
whose HD-audio controllers were enabled in the same millisecond. With a
sink whose ELD speaker allocation is 0x6f (10 speaker positions),
reading the chmap TLV of the NVIDIA HDMI PCM from the ALSA sink of a
media player produced 64 warnings at hdmi_chmap.c:651
(WARN_ON(count != channels) in hdmi_cea_alloc_to_tlv_chmap()) and 8
hardened-usercopy warnings, "Buffer overflow detected (32 < 36)!", from
hdmi_chmap_ctl_tlv(): an entry claimed 9 channels, so chs_bytes
exceeded the 8-entry tlv_chmap[] and the copy was refused. A corrupted
count can also make hdmi_channel_allocation_spk_alloc_blk() select a
channel allocation whose speakers do not match the stream.

Compute the values in locals and store each field once. Concurrent
initialisations then write identical values, and a reader never sees a
partial count.
---
 sound/hda/core/hdmi_chmap.c | 16 ++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/sound/hda/core/hdmi_chmap.c b/sound/hda/core/hdmi_chmap.c
index c897fc4..68a7e81 100644
--- a/sound/hda/core/hdmi_chmap.c
+++ b/sound/hda/core/hdmi_chmap.c
@@ -222,14 +222,22 @@ static void init_channel_allocations(void)
 	struct hdac_cea_channel_speaker_allocation *p;
 
 	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(channel_allocations); i++) {
+		int channels = 0;
+		int spk_mask = 0;
+
 		p = channel_allocations + i;
-		p->channels = 0;
-		p->spk_mask = 0;
 		for (j = 0; j < ARRAY_SIZE(p->speakers); j++)
 			if (p->speakers[j]) {
-				p->channels++;
-				p->spk_mask |= p->speakers[j];
+				channels++;
+				spk_mask |= p->speakers[j];
 			}
+		/*
+		 * Every codec calls this at registration, possibly concurrently
+		 * with another codec's probe or with a TLV read. Store the final
+		 * values once, so the table never holds a partial count.
+		 */
+		WRITE_ONCE(p->channels, channels);
+		WRITE_ONCE(p->spk_mask, spk_mask);
 	}
 }
 
